+      # ----- Cursor / page-number pagination -----
+      #
+      # Legacy clients use `?page=2&per_page=25` (offset pagination).
+      # New clients use `?page[size]=25`, optionally with
+      # `page[after]=<cursor>` or `page[before]=<cursor>`.
+      #
+      # A cursor is an opaque, URL-safe Base64-encoded JSON payload that
+      # stores the values of the collection's ordering columns for a
+      # record (e.g. [updated_at, id] for tickets, which are ordered by
+      # most-recent activity, or [created_at, id] for comments, or just
+      # [id] for ID-ordered collections).  Because the next page is
+      # located by those values rather than by an OFFSET, inserting,
+      # updating or deleting records before the cursor does not shift
+      # the window: already-seen records are not repeated and unseen
+      # records are not skipped.  The trailing `id` acts as a unique
+      # tie-breaker when the leading timestamp column ties.
